{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>A talent of pure gold</b> — that its weight together with all its utensils should be no more and no less than a talent. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 25:39). And so it is the plain sense of the verse. But if the matter is so, there is a great wonder: the verse gives no weight for the menorah — how much of the talent would be in it — and one could give half a talent or more to the tongs and the snuff-dishes, which are utensils separate from it, and diminish from it, or give in the menorah a talent less a mina and in all these utensils a mina. Furthermore, what reason is there that the menorah should be weighed together with many separate utensils at the weight of the talent, and why were the tongs and snuff-dishes not specified — how much they would be made from it?\n\nBut the opinion of our Sages is not as the Rav said. Rather, thus the Sages taught in Menahot (88b): The menorah and its lamps come from a talent, but not its tongs and its snuff-dishes. And how do I fulfill <b>אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים הָאֵלֶּה</b> (\"all these utensils\")? To include the lamps — these are the words of Rabbi Yehudah. Rabbi Nechemiah says: the menorah comes from a talent, but not its lamps, its tongs, and its snuff-dishes. And how do I fulfill <b>אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים הָאֵלֶּה</b>? That they should all be of gold. And there they said: according to Rabbi Yehudah, the lamps were hammered out (מקשה) together with it, and even so Scripture calls them \"these utensils,\" because they are utensils for receiving the oil, and they have a name in their own right, and in the case of other menorahs they are made separate from it. And according to Rabbi Nechememiah they said there that the lamps are not hammered out with it. Now according to Rabbi Yehudah's view, nothing enters the weight of the talent except the body of the menorah with whatever is hammered out together with it — not separate utensils at all. And according to Rabbi Nechemiah they said there: And how do I fulfill <b>אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים הָאֵלֶּה</b>? That they should all be of gold, and this was needed only for the lamps.\n\nAnd according to the way of peshat, one would say: <b>כִּכָּר זָהָב טָהוֹר יַעֲשֶׂה אֹתָהּ</b> (\"a talent of pure gold shall he make it\"), <b>אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים הָאֵלֶּה</b> (\"all these utensils\") he shall make of pure gold — for initially it did not specify regarding the lamps that they should be of gold at all. And similarly in the account of the making it says (Exodus 37:23): \"And he made its seven lamps, and its tongs, and its snuff-dishes, of pure gold,\" and \"pure gold\" refers back only to the tongs and the snuff-dishes. And it reverted and said (ibid. v. 24): \"a talent of pure gold he made it and all its utensils\" — that he made all its utensils of pure gold but not from the talent. And all its utensils would further include the oil vessels, since he made for it many utensils besides those mentioned, as it says in the passages of the journeys (Numbers 4:9): \"and they shall cover the lampstand of the light, and its lamps, and its tongs, and its snuff-dishes, and all the oil vessels with which they serve it.\" And the verse that says (ibid. 8:4): \"down to its base, down to its flower, it is hammered (מקשה),\" according to its plain meaning it appears thus: its body is hammered, but not its lamps. And all this is according to the position of the Gemara.\n\nBut in the baraita of Melakhet ha-Mishkan (Ozar Midrashim 9, p. 101b) it is taught differently: The menorah that Moses made in the wilderness was of gold, and required hammering (מקשה), and required cups, knobs, and flowers, as it says (Exodus 25:31): \"וְעָשִׂיתָ מְנֹרַת זָהָב טָהוֹר\" (\"and you shall make a lampstand of pure gold\"). Might I say he should make it limb by limb and join them together? Scripture says: \"מִמֶּנָּה יִהְיוּ\" (\"from it they shall be,\" Exodus 25:36). From where do I include its lamps, that they should be from it? Scripture says: \"יַעֲשֶׂה\" (\"he shall make,\" in the verse before us). Could it be that I include its cups, its knobs, and its flowers? Scripture says: \"אֹתָהּ\" (\"it,\" ibid.). And what did you see to include its lamps and exclude its cups, its knobs, and its flowers? After all, the verse included and then excluded — I include its lamps, which are made together with it, and I exclude its cups, its knobs, and its flowers, which are not made together with it. And from where to include its tongs and its snuff-dishes? Scripture says: \"יַעֲשֶׂה\" (ibid.). Could it be that I include the tongs (צבתים) and the melikta'ot? Scripture says: \"אֹתָהּ\" (ibid.). And what did you see to include its tongs and its snuff-dishes and exclude the tongs (צבתים) and the melikta'ot? After all, the verse included and then excluded — I include its tongs and its snuff-dishes, which are used together with it, and I exclude the tongs (צבתים) and the melikta'ot, which are not used together with it. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Karchah says: \"it\" — from a talent, but its utensils are not from a talent, as it says: \"כִּכָּר זָהָב טָהוֹר יַעֲשֶׂה אֹתָהּ\" (\"a talent of pure gold shall he make it\"). And how do I fulfill \"וְאֵת כָּל כֵּלֶיהָ\" (\"and all its utensils,\" below 37:24)? That they should be of pure gold. End of the baraita.\n\nFrom it it appears that the lamps and the tongs and the snuff-dishes were all hammered together with it, and the melikachayim are not tongs (צבתים); rather, he made upon the mouths of the lamps a plate (טס) of gold that opens and closes and rests upon it so that nothing falls into the oil, as is done today with the menorahs of kings. And this is from the language of \"וּלְשׁוֹנִי מֻדְבָּק מַלְקֹחָי\" (\"and my tongue cleaves to my jaws,\" Psalms 22:16), so that what is above the tongue and beneath it is called מלקוחים, because they grasp the tongue in their midst. And the mahatot are cups beneath each and every lamp to catch the sparks of fire that fall from it, and all are hammered and come from the talent. But the tongs (צבתים) and the melukotim, whose use is not for it itself but are separate utensils from it and are not attached to it, do not come from the talent at all. And likewise they excluded in this baraita its cups, its knobs, and its flowers: if he wished not to make them hammered together with it, he is permitted. And they gave the reason: because they are not made together with it — that is, they are not indispensable to it, for if he made it of other kinds of metal, it does not require cups, knobs, and flowers (Menahot 28a). But the branches (קנים) are always indispensable to it. And according to this baraita, Scripture says \"וְאֵת כָּל כֵּלֶיהָ\" (Exodus 37:24) to include that he made for it all the utensils needed for its use and fitting for it — of gold — and these are the tongs (צבתים) and the melikta'ot, which are needed for all menorahs in their use. And similarly \"לְכֹל כְּלֵי הַמִּשְׁכָּן\" etc. (Exodus 27:19) — utensils needed for the Mishkan; he did not specify them and mentioned of them only that they should be of bronze. But <b>כִּכָּר זָהָב טָהוֹר יַעֲשֶׂה אֹתָהּ אֵת כָּל הַכֵּלִים הָאֵלֶּה</b> — these are those mentioned in connection with it, and all are hammered together with it. And it is possible that what is also said in the account of the making, \"וְאֵת כָּל כֵּלֶיהָ\" (Exodus 37:24), alludes to these utensils mentioned in connection with it, that they were all together with it — a talent of gold — according to the plain sense of the verse. But the oil vessels were not mentioned in the account of the making, just as they were not mentioned in the command, and they made them of their own volition; or perhaps Bezalel did not make them, but individuals made them and delivered them to the community. And in general, its body comes from a talent, and according to all opinions, nothing outside its body comes from the talent."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The baraita from Melakhet ha-Mishkan is cited from Ozar Midrashim (Eisenstein), and the page reference 'ש\"א ב' likely means 101b in that edition; rendered as given. The terms צבתים and מלקטאות/מלוקטים are left transliterated in places where they appear as technical designations in the baraita's argument, since Ramban distinguishes them from the מלקחים of the verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:17:18.635216891Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}